Now the battlefield: with zoom 0.4895 and centered camera, the right worms are still partially clipped! In the screenshot, at right edge x~740-780 (2x) there are two purple worms, one at x~660 and one at x~760, with the rightmost half cut. Why? The camera centered at 900, right worm at 1252 → screen x = 195 + (1252-900)*.4895 = 367.3 CSS = 734.6 at 2x. Worm sprite width maybe 40 world * .4895 = 19.6 CSS, so extends to 377 CSS = 754 at 2x. But the screenshot shows a purple worm at ~760-780 partially cut. Wait, there are two enemy worms: VIPER at 1252 and MOSS at 1148. The rightmost at 1252 should be at 367 CSS. In the screenshot at 2x, the rightmost purple worm is at x~755-780 (377-390 CSS) — that's 1252? Maybe camera is not centered because active worm is BISCUIT at 548, nearest enemy is 1148 (MOSS), midpoint 848, not 900! Ah, `nearestEnemy` returns the nearest enemy to active: from 548, nearest is 1148 (MOSS), not 1252 (VIPER). So midpoint = (548+1148)/2 = 848. Then screen right worm 1252 → 195+(1252-848)*.4895=392.7 → clipped. Yes.

We need to center on the **team centroids** or the full squad bounds, not nearest enemy. In portrait, center on the midpoint of all alive worms, or min/max x of all alive worms. Let's use squad bounds: minX/maxX of all alive worms, center = (min+max)/2. That's 900. During AI turn same. During firing/settle camera follows projectile/hit, fine. During aim, maybe also consider the active worm and target? Use all alive worms. This ensures both squads visible. Implement:
